/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Science (batch z) */
WB.L("states-of-matter", "science", "beginner", "Solid, liquid, gas … and stranger things",
  "School taught us three states of matter. Nature has more, including one that fills the stars and one that was only made in a lab in 1995.",
  [
    ["Particles on the move", "Solids, liquids and gases differ in how freely their particles move.",
      "Everything is made of atoms and molecules that never stop moving. In a solid, they are locked in place and can only vibrate, which is why a brick keeps its shape. In a liquid, they are still packed closely but can slide past one another, so water takes the shape of its container. In a gas, they fly about freely, bouncing off each other and the walls, with mostly empty space between them. The molecules in the air you are breathing travel at roughly 500 metres per second, faster than a passenger jet, colliding with each other billions of times every second."],
    ["Changing state", "Substances melt, boil or sublimate when their particles gain enough energy to break free, and boiling depends on air pressure.",
      "Heat a solid and its particles vibrate harder until they break out of their fixed places and it melts. Keep heating and some escape as a gas. While ice melts, its temperature stays at 0°C, because the energy goes into breaking the bonds holding particles in place rather than making them move faster.\n\nBoiling also depends on the air pressure above. Water boils at 100°C at sea level but only around 70°C on the summit of Mount Everest, which makes a decent cup of tea surprisingly hard. Some substances skip the liquid stage. Dry ice, which is frozen carbon dioxide, turns straight into gas, a change called sublimation."],
    ["Glass and pitch", "Glass is a rigid amorphous solid that does not flow, whereas pitch really is an extremely slow liquid.",
      "Glass has jumbled atoms like a liquid yet is rigid, so scientists call it an amorphous solid. The story that old windows are thicker at the bottom because glass flowed over centuries is a myth. Old panes were spun or blown unevenly, and glaziers often fitted the thick edge down. At room temperature, glass would take far longer than the age of the universe to sag visibly.\n\nPitch, a tar-like substance that shatters under a hammer, truly flows. In 1927 Professor Thomas Parnell at the University of Queensland poured heated pitch into a funnel and, three years later, cut its stem open. A drop takes the best part of a decade to fall, and fewer than a dozen have. A camera set up in 2000 failed at the crucial moment."],
    ["Plasma and condensates", "Plasma is a gas so hot its atoms are torn apart, while a Bose–Einstein condensate forms just above absolute zero.",
      "Heat a gas enough and collisions knock electrons off their atoms. The result is plasma, a mix of free electrons and positive ions that conducts electricity and responds strongly to magnetic fields. Lightning, neon signs, auroras and every star, including the Sun, are plasma, so most of the ordinary visible matter in the universe is thought to be in this state.\n\nAt the other extreme, Bose and Einstein predicted in the 1920s that atoms cooled near absolute zero would fall into one quantum state and act as a single giant wave. In 1995 Eric Cornell and Carl Wieman in Colorado cooled rubidium atoms to around 170 billionths of a degree above absolute zero and made one. They shared the 2001 Nobel Prize in Physics with Wolfgang Ketterle."],
    ["The in-betweens", "Solid, liquid and gas are human labels, and many materials refuse to fit neatly into one.",
      "Mix cornflour with water and you get oobleck, a goo that pours like a liquid but turns firm when you punch it or run across it. Scientists call it a non-Newtonian fluid, because its thickness changes with the force applied. Liquid crystals flow like a liquid but line up in an orderly way like a crystal, and turning them with electric fields is how many screens control light. Sand and grain can pour through a funnel like water, yet hold up a person standing on a beach. Nature is under no obligation to follow the labels people invented."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "In which state are particles packed closely but able to slide past one another?", ["Solid","Liquid","Gas","Plasma"], 1, "Liquid particles touch but are not locked in place, so liquids take the shape of their container."],
    [1, "Why does water boil at a lower temperature on top of Mount Everest?", ["The air pressure is much lower","The water there is purer","The air contains less oxygen","The mountain's rocks are colder"], 0, "Boiling depends on the pressure above the liquid, and the air at that height presses far less."],
    [2, "Why are some old window panes thicker at the bottom?", ["Glass slowly flows downwards over centuries","Gravity gradually compresses glass over time","They were made unevenly and fitted thick edge down","Rain leaves mineral deposits along the bottom edge"], 2, "Glass at room temperature does not flow noticeably, so the uneven thickness came from how old panes were made."],
    [3, "What is plasma?", ["A very thick liquid found inside stars","A gas hot enough that its atoms split into electrons and ions","A solid with no regular crystal structure","A gas cooled to just above absolute zero"], 1, "Extreme heat strips electrons from atoms, and the charged mixture behaves unlike an ordinary gas."],
    [4, "Why does oobleck count as an in-between material?", ["It melts at room temperature","It is made from only one type of atom","Its thickness changes with the force applied to it","It alternates between a gas and a solid"], 2, "A non-Newtonian fluid like oobleck pours like a liquid but turns firm under sudden force."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Ice stays at 0 degrees while it melts, even as you heat it. Where does the energy go?"});

WB.L("air-pressure", "science", "beginner", "Air pressure: the weight of the sky",
  "Right now the air is pressing on you with the weight of about ten tonnes on every square metre. Here's why you don't notice, and how people first worked it out.",
  [
    ["An ocean of air", "Air has weight, and the atmosphere presses on everything, but we are not crushed because the pressure is balanced.",
      "Air feels like nothing, but it is made of real molecules with real mass, and there are many kilometres of it stacked above your head. At sea level, that weight presses on every surface with a force of roughly ten tonnes per square metre, about 101 kilopascals. So why aren't we flattened? Because air pressure pushes in every direction at once, not just downward, and the fluids and air inside your body push back outward with equal force. Everything is balanced. You only notice when that balance is suddenly upset, for example when your ears pop in a lift, or when a sealed packet of chips puffs up on a flight."],
    ["Torricelli's tube", "Torricelli showed that the weight of the air can hold up a column of mercury, which led to the barometer.",
      "In the early 1600s, well diggers and engineers knew a frustrating fact. Suction pumps could not raise water more than about ten metres, however well they were built. Evangelista Torricelli, a former assistant to Galileo, suspected the weight of the air was the answer. In 1643 he filled a long glass tube with mercury, which is about 13.6 times denser than water, covered the open end and turned it upside down into a dish of mercury. The column dropped until it stood about 76 centimetres high, leaving an empty space at the top. The air pressing on the dish was holding the mercury up. Torricelli had made the first barometer, and he noticed the height changed slightly from day to day."],
    ["Up the mountain", "A barometer carried up a mountain showed lower pressure at height, proving that pressure comes from the air above.",
      "The French thinker Blaise Pascal reasoned that if the atmosphere really had weight, less of it should press down at the top of a mountain. In 1648 he persuaded his brother-in-law, Florin Périer, to test the idea on the Puy de Dôme, a volcanic peak in central France. Périer left one mercury tube at the bottom with a watcher and carried another to the summit, about a kilometre higher. At the top, the mercury column was several centimetres shorter. Coming down, it rose again. It was a beautifully simple experiment that settled the question. Today the unit of pressure, the pascal, honours Pascal, and aircraft altimeters still work by measuring this same fall in pressure with height."],
    ["A push, not a pull", "What looks like suction is really the atmosphere pushing, because a vacuum cannot pull anything.",
      "Around 1650, Otto von Guericke, scientist and mayor of Magdeburg, built one of the first air pumps. He fitted two copper hemispheres about half a metre across together with only a greased rim between them, then pumped out the air. The atmosphere outside clamped the halves together. Accounts describe two teams of horses failing to pull the sphere apart, yet when air was let back in, the halves simply fell open.\n\nIt is the same when you drink through a straw. You lower the pressure inside your mouth, and the normal air pressure on the drink's surface pushes it up. Suction cups work the same way. That also explains the old pump puzzle: the atmosphere can push water up only about ten metres, because that is the height of water column it can balance."],
    ["Pressure in daily life", "Differences in air pressure drive the weather, speed up cooking and shape how aircraft cabins work.",
      "Air pressure changes from place to place, and those differences drive the weather. High pressure usually brings sinking air and settled, clear skies, while low pressure brings rising air, cloud and often rain. Wind is air moving from higher pressure towards lower, which is why the curved lines on a weather map, called isobars, are so useful.\n\nPressure also changes how we cook. A pressure cooker traps steam, raising the pressure so water boils at around 120°C and food cooks much faster. And at an airliner's cruising height, the outside air is far too thin to breathe for long, so cabins are pressurised to a level similar to being on a mountain a couple of kilometres high."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why aren't we crushed by the weight of the atmosphere?", ["Air is too light to matter at ground level","Gravity cancels most of the air pressure out","Our skin is strong enough to resist the force","Air pushes in all directions and our bodies push back"], 3, "Pressure inside and outside the body is balanced, so we feel nothing until that balance is upset."],
    [1, "What did Torricelli's mercury tube experiment show?", ["Air has no weight at all","The weight of the air can hold up a column of mercury","Mercury evaporates in a vacuum","Water is denser than mercury"], 1, "The mercury stayed up because the atmosphere pressed on the dish, which made the first barometer."],
    [2, "What happened to the mercury column at the top of the Puy de Dôme?", ["It was taller, as the air was colder there","It stayed exactly the same","It was shorter, with less air above","It froze solid in the cold"], 2, "Less atmosphere above means lower pressure, which holds up a shorter mercury column."],
    [3, "What actually moves a drink up a straw?", ["Your mouth pulling the drink upward","Air pressure pushing on the drink's surface","The vacuum attracting the liquid","Surface tension inside the straw"], 1, "A vacuum cannot pull, so the surrounding atmosphere does the pushing."],
    [4, "What does low air pressure usually bring?", ["Sinking air and clear, settled skies","Stronger winds that blow only upward","Rising air, cloud and often rain","Lower boiling points in kitchens"], 2, "Low pressure means rising air, which tends to form cloud and rain."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Ten tonnes of air press on every square metre around you. Why aren't you flattened?"});

WB.L("viruses", "science", "beginner", "Viruses: life's tiny hijackers",
  "They can't eat, grow or reproduce on their own, yet they outnumber the stars in the observable universe and have left their fingerprints all over your DNA.",
  [
    ["Genes in a coat", "A virus is genetic instructions in a protein coat that hijacks a cell to copy itself.",
      "A virus is astonishingly simple: a short set of genetic instructions, in DNA or RNA, inside a protein shell called a capsid. Some, like flu and coronaviruses, add a fatty envelope taken from the last cell they infected. It has no machinery to make energy, build proteins or copy itself, and most are far smaller than bacteria, measured in tens to hundreds of nanometres.\n\nSo it hijacks a cell. Surface proteins fit particular receptors on particular cells, like a key in a lock. The COVID-19 virus uses its spike protein to latch onto a receptor called ACE2. Inside, the cell reads the viral genes as its own and builds new viruses, which burst out or bud off, ready to infect neighbours. One cell can release thousands."],
    ["Slipping through the filter", "Viruses were discovered because they passed through filters fine enough to trap bacteria.",
      "In 1892, the Russian scientist Dmitri Ivanovsky studied a disease that left tobacco leaves blotchy. He passed sap from sick plants through a fine porcelain filter meant to trap bacteria. To his surprise, the filtered sap could still infect healthy plants. In 1898 the Dutch microbiologist Martinus Beijerinck repeated the work and concluded that something new was at play, an infectious agent much smaller than any bacterium that could only multiply inside living cells. He used the Latin word virus, meaning poison or slimy liquid. Tobacco mosaic virus became the first virus identified, and later one of the first photographed, once electron microscopes produced clear images in the late 1930s."],
    ["Alive or not?", "Viruses evolve like living things but cannot metabolise or reproduce alone, so scientists still debate whether they are alive.",
      "Outside a cell, a virus is inert. It doesn't eat, breathe, grow or respond to anything, and some can sit on a surface like a speck of dust. Many textbook definitions of life require an organism to run its own metabolism and reproduce by itself, and viruses fail both tests.\n\nYet inside a cell they multiply furiously, and they clearly evolve by natural selection, the hallmark of living systems. Their genes mutate, and the versions best at spreading come to dominate, which is why new flu vaccines are needed most years. Some biologists place viruses on the edge of life, while others argue they deserve a branch of their own. There is no single agreed answer."],
    ["Bacteria-eaters and giants", "Most viruses on Earth infect bacteria, and a few giant viruses are bigger than some bacteria.",
      "The vast majority of viruses infect bacteria, not us. These are called bacteriophages, meaning bacteria eaters, and many look like tiny lunar landers, with a head and spidery legs that inject DNA into a bacterium. Scientists estimate there are around ten million trillion trillion viruses on Earth, far more than the stars in the observable universe. Most float in the oceans, where they kill a large share of marine bacteria every day, releasing nutrients that feed other life.\n\nThen there are the giants. Mimivirus, identified in 2003 inside amoebae, was so large that researchers first mistook it for a bacterium. It even carries genes once thought unique to cellular life."],
    ["Viruses and us", "About eight per cent of human DNA is viral leftovers, while vaccines and antivirals, not antibiotics, protect us from viruses.",
      "Retroviruses, such as HIV, insert their genes into the DNA of the cells they infect. When ancestors' egg or sperm cells were infected, those genes passed to every later generation. Roughly eight per cent of the human genome is now these viral fossils, far more than the small fraction that codes for proteins. Most are silent, but a retrovirus-derived gene called syncytin helps form a key layer of the placenta.\n\nAntibiotics attack structures that bacteria have and viruses lack, so they do nothing against a cold or flu and only breed resistant bacteria. Instead we rely on the immune system, vaccines and antiviral drugs. Smallpox, which killed hundreds of millions of people in the twentieth century, was declared eradicated in 1980."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why can't a virus reproduce on its own?", ["It lacks genetic material of its own","It cannot make energy or proteins itself","It is too large to enter a cell","It is destroyed whenever it meets air"], 1, "A virus carries instructions only, so it must borrow a cell's machinery to make copies."],
    [1, "What first suggested to scientists that something smaller than bacteria could cause disease?", ["It was seen under a light microscope","It glowed under ultraviolet light","It grew on a dish of nutrient jelly","Filtered sap could still infect healthy plants"], 3, "The infectious agent passed through a filter fine enough to stop bacteria, so it had to be smaller."],
    [2, "Why do many scientists hesitate to call viruses fully alive?", ["They cannot mutate or evolve at all","They carry no genetic material of any kind","They cannot run their own metabolism or reproduce","They are never found inside living cells"], 2, "Viruses evolve like living things but depend entirely on a host cell to function and reproduce."],
    [3, "Which kind of virus is by far the most common on Earth?", ["Those that infect bacteria","Those that infect humans","Those that infect plants","Those that infect fish"], 0, "Bacteriophages vastly outnumber viruses that infect animals, and they shape marine life."],
    [4, "Why won't antibiotics cure the flu?", ["Flu is too mild for any treatment to work","Flu viruses are too big for the drugs","Viruses lack what antibiotics attack","Antibiotics only work against fungi"], 2, "Antibiotics target features of bacteria, so against viruses they do nothing and encourage resistance."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Viruses may not be alive, yet they've left their mark in your DNA. Here's how."});

WB.L("why-things-float", "science", "beginner", "Why things float",
  "A steel ship weighing tens of thousands of tonnes floats, yet a small pebble sinks. The explanation goes back to a Greek genius, and possibly a bath.",
  [
    ["The Eureka legend", "The famous bath story is probably more legend than history, but the principle behind it is real.",
      "The tale goes that King Hiero II of Syracuse suspected his goldsmith had mixed silver into a gold crown. He asked Archimedes to find out without damaging it. Stepping into a bath, Archimedes noticed the water rise, realised he could measure the crown's volume by the water it displaced, and ran through the streets shouting ‘Eureka’, meaning ‘I have found it’.\n\nThe trouble is that the Roman architect Vitruvius wrote the story down about two centuries after Archimedes died, and some historians doubt it. Galileo pointed out that the overflow method would be too imprecise for a crown, and suggested Archimedes more likely used a balance and his principle of buoyancy. Either way, the principle is enormously useful."],
    ["Archimedes' principle", "An object in a fluid is pushed up by a force equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces.",
      "Archimedes wrote a treatise called On Floating Bodies, and its central idea is now known as Archimedes' principle. When an object sits in water, it pushes some water out of the way. The surrounding water pushes back, creating an upward force called buoyancy, equal to the weight of the water displaced. If the object weighs less than the water it can displace, it floats. If it weighs more, it sinks, though it still feels lighter, which is why you can lift a friend easily in a swimming pool. The principle works for any fluid, liquid or gas. It explains why a log bobs on a river and why a helium balloon rises through the heavier air around it."],
    ["How a steel ship floats", "A ship floats because its hull encloses air and displaces a volume of water that weighs as much as the ship.",
      "Steel is about eight times denser than water, so a solid block of it sinks instantly. Hammer the same steel into a bowl shape, though, and it can float. The hull encloses a huge volume of air. As the ship settles into the water, it pushes aside water and sinks lower until the weight of the water displaced exactly equals the ship's own weight. At that point it floats.\n\nThis is also why a ship sits lower when loaded with cargo, because it must push aside more water to balance the extra weight. Punch a hole in the hull and let water replace the air, and the balance is lost."],
    ["Density and safe loading", "The denser the water, the easier it is to float, which is why ships carry several load lines.",
      "In nineteenth century Britain, some owners overloaded old, overinsured ships so heavily that they sank with their crews. Sailors called them coffin ships. MP Samuel Plimsoll campaigned against the practice, and the Merchant Shipping Act of 1876 made load line markings compulsory. The Plimsoll line shows how deep a ship may safely sit. Modern versions have several marks because fresh water is less dense than seawater, and warm water is less dense than cold.\n\nThe same idea explains why you float more easily in the sea than in a pool, and why an egg sinks in tap water but floats in very salty water. The Dead Sea, on the border of Israel, Jordan and the West Bank, is several times saltier than the ocean, so swimmers bob like they're lying on a lilo."],
    ["Submarines, fish and balloons", "Machines and animals rise and sink by changing their density through ballast, swim bladders or heated air.",
      "A submarine floats on the surface until it opens its ballast tanks and lets seawater in. Heavier now, it sinks. To rise, it uses compressed air to blow the water back out. Many bony fish manage the same trick with a swim bladder, a gas-filled sac they inflate or deflate to hover at a chosen depth without constantly swimming.\n\nBuoyancy works in air too. Heating air makes it expand and become less dense, so a hot air balloon is pushed upward by the cooler, denser air around it. On 21 November 1783, a balloon built by the Montgolfier brothers carried two men across Paris, in the first free flight with people on board. Your own body is close to the density of water, which is why a deep breath of air helps you float."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do some historians doubt the famous ‘Eureka’ story?", ["It was recorded about two centuries later","Baths did not exist in ancient Greece","Archimedes never lived in Syracuse","Gold crowns were never made in that era"], 0, "The earliest account comes long after the event, and a crown's volume is hard to measure by overflow."],
    [1, "According to Archimedes' principle, the upward force on an object in a fluid equals what?", ["The volume of the object itself","The weight of the fluid it displaces","The depth of the water around it","The surface area of the object"], 1, "Buoyancy equals the weight of fluid pushed aside, so an object floats if it weighs less than that."],
    [2, "Why can a steel ship float when a solid steel block sinks?", ["Ship steel is lighter than ordinary steel","Seawater is denser than any steel","Its hull encloses air and displaces a lot of water","Its engines hold it up in the water"], 2, "Shape spreads the ship's weight over enough displaced water to balance it."],
    [3, "Why does a ship's Plimsoll line have several marks?", ["Water density changes with saltiness and temperature","Different cargoes each need their own mark","Crew numbers change how low a ship sits","The time of day changes how deep a ship floats"], 0, "Fresh and warm water are less dense than salty or cold water, so a ship sits lower in them."],
    [4, "How does a submarine dive?", ["By spinning its propeller backwards","By letting seawater into its ballast tanks","By pumping air into its tanks","By heating its hull"], 1, "Adding water makes the submarine heavier than the water it displaces, so it sinks."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A steel ship floats while a pebble sinks. The answer is about shape, not weight."});

WB.L("static-electricity", "science", "beginner", "Static electricity: sparks from nowhere",
  "That zap from a car door or a supermarket trolley is the same force that gave electricity its name, and it runs the printer in your office.",
  [
    ["Amber and a word", "The word electricity comes from the Greek word for amber, which attracts small objects when rubbed.",
      "Ancient Greeks noticed that amber, the golden fossilised tree resin, attracted bits of straw, feathers and dust when rubbed with fur or wool. Tradition credits the philosopher Thales of Miletus, around 600 BC, with describing this, though nothing he wrote survives. The Greek word for amber was elektron. Almost two thousand years later, in 1600, the English physician William Gilbert showed in a book on magnetism that many other materials, like glass and sulphur, behaved the same way. He called them electricus, Latin for like amber. From that root came the word electricity, so every power point in your home carries an echo of a lump of ancient resin."],
    ["Where the charge comes from", "Rubbing two materials moves electrons from one to the other, and the charged objects then attract or repel.",
      "Atoms contain positive protons and negative electrons, normally in balance. When two different materials touch and rub, electrons can transfer from one to the other. The one that gains electrons becomes negative and the other positive. Scientists rank materials by this tendency in the triboelectric series. Opposite charges attract and like charges repel.\n\nRub a balloon on your jumper and it sticks to a wall, even though the wall is neutral. The negative balloon pushes electrons in the wall's surface slightly away and draws positive charge closer, and the nearer attraction wins. This is induction. Put your hand on a Van de Graaff generator and your hairs all share one charge, repel and stand up. Physicists still don't fully understand how charge moves in contact."],
    ["Why you get zapped", "Static shocks happen when built-up charge jumps to a conductor, and they are worse in dry air.",
      "Walking across carpet in rubber-soled shoes, or sliding across a car seat, can build up several thousand volts on your body. Nothing happens until you reach for something metal, like a door handle. Then the charge jumps the gap in a tiny spark, and you feel the zap. Despite the high voltage, the shock is harmless because the amount of charge is tiny and the current lasts a fraction of a second.\n\nShocks are far more common in dry conditions, such as cold winter days or air-conditioned offices. In humid air, a thin film of moisture lets charge leak away before it can build up. A simple trick is to touch metal with a key first, so the spark jumps from the key."],
    ["Static at work", "Photocopiers, laser printers, pollution filters and paint sprayers all use static electricity.",
      "In 1938, American patent lawyer Chester Carlson, tired of copying documents by hand, made the first dry copy using static electricity in a makeshift lab in Queens, New York. His process, later called xerography, took years to find a backer but became the basis of the photocopier and the laser printer. Inside, light creates a pattern of charge on a drum, powdery toner sticks only to the charged areas, and heat fuses it onto paper.\n\nStatic also cleans the air. Electrostatic precipitators, developed in the early twentieth century, charge smoke particles in chimneys so they are drawn onto collecting plates. Car makers use the same idea to spray paint evenly onto charged car bodies."],
    ["When static is dangerous", "A tiny static spark can ignite fuel vapour or dust and can quietly destroy electronics.",
      "Most shocks are merely annoying, but a spark in the wrong place can start a fire. Petrol gives off flammable vapour, and fuel stations warn people not to get back into their car while refuelling, because sliding across the seat can build up charge that may spark at the nozzle. Touching the metal body of the car before handling the nozzle helps discharge you safely. If a fire starts, move away and call 000.\n\nFine dust from flour, sugar or grain can explode if a spark ignites a dense cloud of it, which is why factories and silos manage static carefully. Electronics are vulnerable too. A discharge too small for you to feel can still damage a microchip, so technicians often wear grounding wrist straps."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where does the word electricity come from?", ["The Greek word for amber","The Latin word for lightning","The Greek word for spark","The English word for charge"], 0, "Amber attracted small objects when rubbed, and the Greek word for it gave electricity its name."],
    [1, "Why does a charged balloon stick to a neutral wall?", ["The wall carries the opposite charge permanently","Static electricity works like glue on smooth walls","The wall's electrons all jump onto the balloon","Charges in the wall shift and attract the balloon"], 3, "Induction shifts charges inside the wall, leaving opposite charge nearest the balloon."],
    [2, "Why are static shocks more common in dry air?", ["Moisture in humid air drains charge away","Dry air carries more electrons","Cold air makes metal hold more charge","Dry air is a better conductor of charge"], 0, "A thin film of moisture on surfaces drains charge before it can build up into a spark."],
    [3, "How does a laser printer put toner on the page?", ["Heat makes the toner stick to the drum","Toner sticks only to areas of the drum carrying charge","A magnet pulls the toner onto the paper","The toner is dissolved in a wet ink"], 1, "Light makes a charge pattern on the drum, and charged areas pick up toner, which heat then fuses to paper."],
    [4, "Why should you avoid sliding across your car seat while refuelling?", ["It can scratch the seat and the fuel gauge","It can build up a spark near petrol vapour","It makes the pump deliver fuel too fast","It can damage the car's electronic systems"], 1, "A small spark near flammable vapour can start a fire, so touch the car's metal body before handling the nozzle."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The word electricity comes from a lump of golden resin. Here is the story."});

WB.L("plastics-polymers", "science", "intermediate", "Plastics: the miracle material",
  "Plastics went from a Victorian curiosity to something found in the deepest ocean trench in barely a century. It's a story of long molecules, lucky accidents and an unsolved problem.",
  [
    ["Molecules like chains", "Plastics are polymers, giant molecules built from thousands of small repeating units, which chemists can tune.",
      "The word polymer comes from Greek for ‘many parts’. A polymer is a giant molecule made by linking small building blocks, called monomers, into long chains, often thousands of units long. Polyethylene, the plastic in shopping bags and milk bottles, is the small molecule ethylene joined end to end over and over.\n\nNature made polymers long before chemists did. Silk, rubber, wool, the cellulose in wood and paper, and even your own DNA are polymers. What makes synthetic plastics so useful is that chemists can tune them. By choosing different monomers, chain lengths and links between chains, they can make materials that are soft or rigid, clear or coloured, stretchy or tough."],
    ["Plastics by accident and war", "Plastics began with altered natural materials in the 1860s, and several key ones were found by accident before World War II.",
      "The first plastics were chemically altered natural materials. Alexander Parkes showed Parkesine, made from cellulose, in London in 1862. John Wesley Hyatt developed celluloid around 1870, partly in a search for an ivory substitute for billiard balls, and it later became early film, though it was dangerously flammable. In 1907 Leo Baekeland created Bakelite, the first fully synthetic plastic: hard, heat resistant and insulating, it went into radios and telephones.\n\nIn 1933, ICI chemists Eric Fawcett and Reginald Gibson found a waxy solid, polyethylene, in a high-pressure vessel; it soon insulated wartime radar cables. In 1938 Roy Plunkett at DuPont found a slippery powder in an apparently empty cylinder, which became Teflon. Wallace Carothers' nylon stockings sensationally went on sale in 1940, then went to war as parachutes and ropes."],
    ["Two families and codes", "Thermoplastics can be melted and reshaped, thermosets cannot, and the number in the recycling triangle is not a promise of recycling.",
      "Thermoplastics have long tangled chains that slide past one another when heated, so they soften and can be moulded again. Polyethylene, polypropylene, PVC and PET (drink bottles) are thermoplastics, which makes recycling by melting possible. Thermosets, such as Bakelite, epoxy glue and fibreglass resin, form chemical cross-links as they set into one rigid network. They char rather than melt, which makes them tough and very hard to recycle.\n\nThe number from 1 to 7 inside a triangle of arrows, introduced in the United States in 1988, only identifies the plastic: 1 is PET, 2 is high-density polyethylene, 5 is polypropylene, 7 is everything else. It is not a recycling promise. What can be recycled depends on local facilities, so check your council's guidelines."],
    ["Slow to break down", "Plastic is cheap and durable, so it builds up in the environment and only a small share has ever been recycled.",
      "Global plastic production has grown from almost nothing in 1950 to roughly 400 million tonnes a year by the 2020s. A widely cited 2017 study estimated that of all plastic waste generated up to 2015, only around 9 per cent had been recycled, and the rest was burned, buried or left in the environment.\n\nPart of the problem is chemistry. The same strong carbon chains that make plastics durable mean few microbes can digest them, so most do not biodegrade in any useful timeframe. Sunlight and waves slowly break them into smaller fragments instead. Recycling has limits too, because many plastics get shorter chains and weaker each time they are melted and reprocessed."],
    ["Tiny plastics everywhere", "Microplastics are now found almost everywhere, but their health effects are still uncertain.",
      "Plastic pieces smaller than five millimetres are called microplastics. Some are made small, like the microbeads once common in face scrubs, but most come from larger items breaking down, tyres wearing on roads and synthetic fibres shed in the wash. They have been found in Arctic snow, on remote mountaintops and in the deepest part of the ocean, the Mariana Trench. Studies have also detected them in human blood and organs.\n\nWhat that means for health is genuinely uncertain. Researchers are still developing reliable ways to measure such tiny particles and to work out whether, and at what levels, they cause harm. Read early headlines with care."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What makes synthetic plastics so versatile?", ["They contain no carbon atoms at all","Their building blocks and chains can be tuned","They are all made from one natural resin","Their molecules are all the same length"], 1, "Adjusting the building blocks and chain structure produces materials from soft to rigid and clear to coloured."],
    [1, "Why was Bakelite a turning point in plastics?", ["It was the first fully synthetic plastic","It was the first plastic that could float","It was the first plastic to replace ivory","It was the first plastic found by accident"], 0, "Bakelite showed plastics did not have to start from natural materials, and its heat resistance suited electrical fittings."],
    [2, "What does the number inside the recycling triangle tell you?", ["That your council will recycle the item","How many times the item can be recycled","How safe the plastic is to eat from","What type of plastic the item is made of"], 3, "The code identifies the plastic, while actual recycling depends on local facilities."],
    [3, "Why do most plastics persist in the environment for so long?", ["Few microbes can digest their strong carbon chains","They are chemically identical to rock","They are too light to break down","Seawater preserves them"], 0, "Durable carbon chains resist biological breakdown, so sunlight and waves only fragment plastics."],
    [4, "What is the best way to treat headlines about microplastics and health?", ["As proof that they are harmless at any level","As settled fact that they cause specific diseases","As rumours with no basis in research","As early, uncertain findings from ongoing research"], 3, "Scientists are still working out how to measure these particles and whether they cause harm."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"That recycling triangle on plastic doesn't mean what most people think it does."});

WB.L("metric-measurement", "science", "intermediate", "Measuring the world: the metric story",
  "Revolutionaries once set out to measure the planet to define the metre. Today a metre, a second and a kilogram are fixed by the laws of nature themselves.",
  [
    ["From muddle to metre", "The metric system replaced confusing local units with a metre based on the size of the Earth.",
      "In eighteenth century France, units of length, weight and volume varied from region to region and even market to market, so merchants and tax collectors could exploit the confusion. After the Revolution of 1789, reformers wanted a rational system based on nature, belonging to no nation: for all times and all peoples.\n\nIn 1791 the French Academy of Sciences decided the metre would be one ten-millionth of the distance from the North Pole to the equator through Paris. From 1792, astronomers Delambre and Méchain spent around seven years surveying from Dunkirk to Barcelona, through revolution and war. Because of small errors and the Earth's imperfect shape, the official metre came out about 0.2 millimetres short of the intention. It was close enough, and it stuck."],
    ["Units that fit together", "Metric units link to one another and scale in powers of ten, so conversion is just moving a decimal point.",
      "The genius of the metric system is that its units connect. A litre is the volume of a cube ten centimetres on each side, and the kilogram was originally defined as the mass of one litre of water. Everything scales by tens, so converting is a matter of moving a decimal point, and prefixes like kilo, centi and milli work the same way for every unit.\n\nTemperature joined in too. The Celsius scale puts 0 at the freezing point of water and 100 at its boiling point, though the Swedish astronomer Anders Celsius originally set it the other way around in 1742, with 100 for freezing and 0 for boiling. It was flipped shortly after his death."],
    ["From vault to nature", "Standards moved from metal objects to constants of nature, with the kilogram the last to change in 2019.",
      "In 1889 the standard kilogram became a platinum-iridium cylinder, about 39 millimetres tall, kept under three glass bell jars in a vault at Sèvres near Paris. It was called Le Grand K, and by definition it weighed exactly one kilogram. Over decades, copies and original drifted apart by tens of micrograms, roughly a grain of sand, and nobody could say which had changed.\n\nSo scientists chose constants that never change. In 1967 the second became 9,192,631,770 oscillations of radiation from a caesium atom. In 1983 the metre became the distance light travels in a vacuum in 1/299,792,458 of a second. On 20 May 2019 the kilogram was redefined using the Planck constant, measured with devices called Kibble balances, and Le Grand K retired. Any well-equipped laboratory can now reproduce a kilogram."],
    ["Australia goes metric", "Australia moved to metric in the 1970s, though some imperial units linger in everyday life.",
      "Australia moved to decimal currency in 1966, and the Metric Conversion Act of 1970 set the country on course to change its measurements too. Over the following decade, temperatures, distances, weights and volumes all changed over. One of the most visible moments came on 1 July 1974, when road signs switched from miles to kilometres. Today most of the world uses metric for daily life, with the United States the best-known exception.\n\nStill, older units hang on in Australia in surprising places. Television and phone screens are sold by size in inches, tyre pressures are often given in pounds per square inch, and many people still describe a newborn's weight, or their own height, in imperial units."],
    ["When units go wrong", "Mixing up units has destroyed a spacecraft and nearly brought down an airliner.",
      "In 1999, NASA's Mars Climate Orbiter reached Mars and vanished. Software from one team produced thruster data in imperial pound-force seconds, while another team's software expected metric newton-seconds. The small, repeated error steered the probe too close to the planet, where it was destroyed.\n\nIn 1983, an Air Canada Boeing 767 ran out of fuel at cruising height, partly because a fuel calculation had mixed up pounds and kilograms during the airline's switch to metric. The aircraft carried far less fuel than the crew believed. The pilots glided the powerless jet to a disused airbase at Gimli, in Manitoba, and everyone on board survived. It became known as the Gimli Glider."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the original aim for the length of the metre?", ["One ten-millionth of pole to equator","The length of a French king's foot","The distance light travels in a second","One thousandth of the Earth's width"], 0, "Reformers tied the metre to the planet itself, so no nation or king owned the unit."],
    [1, "What makes the metric system easy to use?", ["Its units only measure length and mass","Its units are linked and scale in powers of ten","Each unit has its own naming pattern","It relies on local standards that vary by town"], 1, "Because everything scales by ten, converting means moving a decimal point."],
    [2, "Why was the kilogram redefined in 2019?", ["Its master object was drifting in mass","Le Grand K was destroyed in a vault fire","The old standard had grown too heavy to use","Countries could not agree on which metal to use"], 0, "Defining the kilogram by the Planck constant lets any laboratory reproduce it without a master object."],
    [3, "Which imperial units still linger in everyday Australian life?", ["Miles on road signs","Pounds as the unit for money","Gallons for petrol prices","Inches for screens, psi for tyres"], 3, "Australia converted in the 1970s, but screens, tyre pressure and baby weights still often use imperial units."],
    [4, "What caused the loss of NASA's Mars Climate Orbiter?", ["A fuel leak during its launch phase","A failure of its solar panels in space","A misplaced decimal in the launch date","A mix-up between imperial and metric units"], 3, "Mixed units produced a small repeated error that steered the probe too close to Mars."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"For 130 years, the kilogram was one lump of metal in a French vault. Then it retired."});

WB.L("chaos-butterfly-effect", "science", "intermediate", "Chaos and the butterfly effect",
  "A weather scientist rounded off a number and went to get a coffee. When he came back, he had discovered why the future can never be fully predicted.",
  [
    ["The clockwork universe", "After Newton, many scientists believed that perfect knowledge of the present would allow perfect prediction of the future.",
      "After Isaac Newton showed that simple laws could explain the motions of the planets, the universe began to look like a precise machine. In 1814, the French mathematician Pierre-Simon Laplace described an imaginary intellect that knew the position of every particle and every force acting on them. For such a being, he wrote, nothing would be uncertain, and the future would be as clear as the past. Later writers called it Laplace's demon.\n\nThe idea was not foolish. Astronomers could predict eclipses centuries ahead. It seemed that the only obstacle to predicting anything, including the weather, was gathering enough data and doing enough calculation."],
    ["Poincaré's error", "Henri Poincaré glimpsed chaos in the 1880s when he found that three bodies under gravity can behave in tangled, unpredictable ways.",
      "In the 1880s, King Oscar II of Sweden offered a prize for proof that the solar system was stable in the long run. The French mathematician Henri Poincaré studied a simplified version, three bodies moving under each other's gravity, and won in 1889.\n\nThen, as his paper was being prepared for publication, a serious error was found. Correcting it, Poincaré realised the three-body problem could behave in extraordinarily tangled ways, with tiny changes leading to vastly different orbits. The printed copies had to be recalled and destroyed, reportedly at a cost greater than the prize money. His corrected work is now seen as an early glimpse of chaos theory."],
    ["Lorenz's coffee break", "In 1961 Edward Lorenz found that rounding one number made a computer weather forecast diverge completely.",
      "Edward Lorenz was a meteorologist at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology who had built a simple weather model on an early computer. In 1961, wanting to look at one run more closely, he restarted it partway through, typing in numbers from an earlier printout. The printout had rounded to three decimal places, so instead of 0.506127 he entered 0.506. The difference was about one part in four thousand.\n\nHe went for a coffee while the slow machine worked. When he returned, the new forecast had tracked the old one for a while, then drifted further and further away until the two bore no resemblance. Lorenz realised this was not a computer fault. It was a property of the weather."],
    ["The butterfly effect", "In chaotic systems, tiny differences grow until they change the outcome completely, even though the rules are exact.",
      "Lorenz published in 1963, but his work reached a wide audience after a 1972 talk. Its title, reportedly suggested by a colleague, asked: ‘Does the flap of a butterfly's wings in Brazil set off a tornado in Texas?’ It was a question, not a claim that butterflies control the weather. By coincidence, a plot of his equations looks like a pair of butterfly wings.\n\nA chaotic system is deterministic: the same start always gives the same result, with no randomness in the rules. The catch is sensitive dependence on initial conditions. Tiny differences grow, roughly doubling again and again, until they swamp everything. Since we can never measure perfectly, predictions eventually fail. Release a double pendulum twice from what looks like the same position and the swings soon diverge wildly."],
    ["Living with chaos", "Chaos limits long-range prediction, but forecasters cope by running many slightly different simulations.",
      "Chaos explains why weather forecasts are reliable for a few days, useful for about a week, and of little value beyond about two weeks. Forecasters deal with this honestly through ensembles, running a model many times with slightly different starting conditions. If the runs agree, confidence is high; if they spread out, the outlook is uncertain.\n\nChaos turns up elsewhere too. In 1976, the Australian-born scientist Robert May showed that a very simple equation for animal populations could swing from steady numbers to wild booms and busts. Even planetary orbits are thought to be chaotic over tens of millions of years. Laplace's demon would need infinitely precise data, and nature never provides that."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Laplace's imaginary intellect illustrate?", ["That the future is knowable if every particle is known","That weather forecasts would soon be perfect","That predicting eclipses is impossible","That the universe has no laws"], 0, "It captured the clockwork view that enough data and calculation would make everything predictable."],
    [1, "What did Poincaré realise when he corrected his error?", ["The solar system is perfectly stable","Three bodies can move in tangled, sensitive ways","Gravity does not act between planets","Three bodies always settle into a fixed orbit"], 1, "Even simple gravity between three bodies can be wildly sensitive to its starting conditions."],
    [2, "What did Lorenz's rounding of a number show?", ["His computer had a hardware fault","Rounding errors always cancel out later","Weather can be forecast years ahead","Tiny changes can grow enormously over time"], 3, "The divergence was a property of the weather model, not a computer fault."],
    [3, "What does it mean to call a chaotic system deterministic?", ["Its outcomes are random","Its rules contain no randomness","It can always be forecast far ahead","It never changes over time"], 1, "Chaos is exact rules combined with sensitivity to tiny differences, not randomness."],
    [4, "How do forecasters handle chaos in the weather?", ["They run a model many times from slightly different starts","They ignore every forecast beyond one day","They replace models with fixed seasonal averages","They wait for more accurate calendars to appear"], 0, "Agreement across an ensemble means high confidence, while a wide spread signals an uncertain outlook."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A rounded number and a coffee break changed how we understand the future."});

WB.L("carbon-element", "science", "intermediate", "Carbon: the element of everything",
  "The same atom makes a diamond, a pencil, a soccer-ball-shaped molecule and most of you. Carbon's secret is its talent for holding hands.",
  [
    ["The great connector", "Carbon forms four strong bonds, so it can build the chains and rings that underpin the molecules of life.",
      "Each carbon atom has four electrons available for bonding, so it can link with up to four other atoms. Crucially, it bonds strongly with other carbon atoms, forming long chains, branches and rings that act as frameworks for other elements. That flexibility makes carbon the backbone of proteins, fats, sugars and DNA. The chemistry of carbon compounds is so vast that it has its own branch, organic chemistry, covering many millions of known substances.\n\nCarbon is common too. After hydrogen, helium and oxygen, it is the fourth most abundant element in the universe by mass, and it makes up around a fifth of your body by mass."],
    ["Born in stars", "Carbon is forged in stars by fusing three helium nuclei, a process Fred Hoyle predicted must be unusually efficient.",
      "The Big Bang produced almost no carbon. It is forged inside stars, where three helium nuclei fuse into one carbon nucleus. In the early 1950s, the British astronomer Fred Hoyle realised that by the known physics this reaction should be far too rare to make the carbon we see. Since carbon obviously exists, he reasoned, carbon nuclei must have a particular energy state that made the reaction much more likely. In 1953 he persuaded sceptical experimental physicists at Caltech to look for it, and they found it close to where he had predicted. The carbon in your cells was made this way, in stars that lived and died long before the Sun formed."],
    ["Diamond, graphite and ‘lead’", "Diamond and graphite are both pure carbon, and only the arrangement of atoms makes one hard and the other soft.",
      "In diamond, each carbon atom is locked to four neighbours in a rigid 3D framework, making it the hardest natural material known. Natural diamonds form over a hundred kilometres down in the mantle, under enormous heat and pressure, and rise in rare volcanic eruptions. In graphite, atoms bond to three neighbours in flat hexagon sheets, stacked like pages and held together weakly, so they slide and graphite is soft and slippery. At the surface graphite is more stable, so diamonds are technically turning into it, far too slowly to notice.\n\nPencils contain no lead. A pure graphite deposit found at Borrowdale, England, in the sixteenth century looked like lead ore, so it was called black lead. Pencil ‘lead’ is graphite mixed with clay: more clay makes a harder H pencil, more graphite a softer B pencil."],
    ["Buckyballs and graphene", "Carbon also forms soccer-ball-shaped molecules and single-atom sheets, both discovered in recent decades.",
      "In 1985, Harold Kroto, Robert Curl and Richard Smalley blasted graphite with a laser and found a stable molecule of exactly 60 carbon atoms: a hollow sphere of 12 pentagons and 20 hexagons, like a soccer ball. They named it buckminsterfullerene, after Buckminster Fuller's geodesic domes, or buckyball. The three shared the 1996 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Buckyballs have since been detected in space.\n\nGraphene is a single sheet of graphite, one atom thick. In 2004 at the University of Manchester, Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov pressed sticky tape onto graphite and kept peeling flakes until some were one atom thick. Graphene is strong, flexible, nearly transparent and conducts well. They won the 2010 Nobel Prize in Physics."],
    ["Carbon on the move", "Carbon cycles between air, oceans, rocks and living things, and burning fossil fuels releases it far faster than nature removes it.",
      "The carbon in your body has been on a long journey. Plants pull carbon dioxide from the air and use sunlight to build it into sugars. Animals eat the plants, and both release carbon dioxide as they breathe and decay. The oceans absorb vast amounts of carbon dioxide, and over millions of years carbon is locked away in limestone and other rocks, then slowly returned by volcanoes.\n\nFossil fuels such as coal, oil and gas are the remains of ancient living things, carbon buried and stored for millions of years. Burning them releases that carbon into the air far faster than natural processes can remove it, which is the main reason carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere are rising."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is carbon the backbone of the molecules of life?", ["It is the most common element in the universe","It forms strong bonds that build chains and rings","It is the lightest element that can bond","It can only bond with hydrogen atoms"], 1, "Carbon's four bonds let it build flexible frameworks for proteins, fats, sugars and DNA."],
    [1, "Where is the carbon in your body made?", ["In the Big Bang","In the Earth's core","In volcanoes","Inside stars, where helium nuclei fuse"], 3, "The Big Bang made almost no carbon, so it was forged in earlier stars."],
    [2, "What makes diamond hard and graphite soft when both are pure carbon?", ["Diamond contains traces of other elements","Graphite is a different element","The arrangement of the atoms differs","Diamond is a liquid at room temperature"], 2, "Diamond's rigid 3D bonds resist force, while graphite's weakly held sheets slide past one another."],
    [3, "What is graphene?", ["A hollow cage of 60 carbon atoms","A single-atom-thick sheet of graphite","A mixture of graphite and clay","A form of diamond found in volcanoes"], 1, "Geim and Novoselov isolated one-atom-thick layers using sticky tape."],
    [4, "Why are atmospheric carbon dioxide levels rising?", ["Plants have stopped absorbing carbon dioxide","Oceans now release more than they absorb","Burning fossil fuels adds carbon too quickly","Volcanoes now erupt far more often than before"], 2, "Fossil fuels stored carbon for millions of years, and we release it within decades."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A diamond and a pencil are made of the same atom. So why are they so different?"});

WB.L("antimatter", "science", "advanced", "Antimatter: the universe's missing twin",
  "For every particle there is a mirror-image twin with the opposite charge. When they meet, both vanish in a flash of pure energy. So why is the universe made almost entirely of one kind?",
  [
    ["Predicted, then found", "Antimatter was predicted by an equation in 1931 and spotted in cosmic-ray tracks in 1932.",
      "In 1928 Paul Dirac combined quantum mechanics with Einstein's special relativity in one equation for the electron. It worked beautifully, but it had a strange feature. Just as the square root of four can be plus or minus two, the equation also gave solutions that seemed to describe electrons with negative energy. Dirac took them seriously. In 1931 he proposed they pointed to a new particle with an electron's mass but a positive charge.\n\nIn 1932 Carl Anderson found it. His cloud chamber, sitting in a strong magnet, recorded cosmic rays from space. He photographed a track that curved like an electron's, but the wrong way. A lead plate across the chamber showed which way the particle travelled. He called it the positron, and shared the 1936 Nobel Prize in Physics."],
    ["Total conversion", "When matter meets its antimatter twin, all of their mass turns into energy.",
      "Every particle has an antiparticle with the same mass but opposite charge. Electrons have positrons, and protons have antiprotons. When a particle meets its antiparticle, they annihilate. Their mass becomes energy, usually as gamma rays, following E = mc².\n\nIt is the most complete conversion of mass into energy known. Nuclear fission and fusion turn less than one per cent of their fuel's mass into energy. Annihilation converts all of it. The process also runs backwards. A sufficiently energetic gamma ray passing near an atomic nucleus can turn into an electron and a positron. This is called pair production, and it creates matter out of energy."],
    ["Building anti-atoms", "Physicists can make anti-atoms and hold them in magnetic traps, and antihydrogen falls under gravity like ordinary matter.",
      "Emilio Segrè and Owen Chamberlain led the team that created the antiproton at Berkeley in 1955, and won the 1959 Nobel Prize in Physics. In 1995 researchers at CERN, near Geneva, combined an antiproton with a positron to make antihydrogen, the simplest anti-atom. They made only a handful, and the atoms annihilated almost instantly.\n\nHolding antimatter is the hard part, because touching the wall of any container destroys it. In 2010 CERN's ALPHA experiment trapped antihydrogen in a carefully shaped magnetic field, and soon held some for around a quarter of an hour. In 2023 ALPHA showed that antihydrogen falls under gravity, just like ordinary matter."],
    ["Useful, but not fuel", "Antimatter helps medical imaging, but making it costs far more energy than it releases, so it cannot be a fuel.",
      "Antimatter already works in hospitals. A PET scan (positron emission tomography) uses a sugar tagged with a radioactive atom. Busy tissue, such as some tumours, takes up more of it. Each emitted positron meets an electron almost at once, and the two annihilate into gamma rays heading in opposite directions. Detectors catch both and map the activity. Positrons also occur naturally, in a very small fraction of potassium-40 decays (found in bananas and in you) and in some thunderstorms.\n\nAs fuel, antimatter disappoints. It is made in accelerators one collision at a time, using far more energy than it could ever return, and it must be suspended in a vacuum. CERN has noted that all the antimatter it has made would light one bulb for only minutes."],
    ["The missing antimatter", "The Big Bang should have made equal matter and antimatter, yet a tiny excess of matter survived and nobody knows why.",
      "Here is the deepest puzzle. Our best theories say the Big Bang should have produced matter and antimatter in equal amounts. They should have annihilated completely, leaving a universe of nothing but light. Instead, there are galaxies, stars and people, all made of matter. A small excess of matter survived, often estimated at about one extra particle for every billion pairs.\n\nIn 1967 Andrei Sakharov set out the conditions needed for such an imbalance. Since 1964, experiments have found that some particles and their antiparticles behave slightly differently, but the differences found so far are far too small to explain it. Why anything exists at all is still unsolved."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Dirac propose a new particle?", ["Cosmic rays had already revealed an unknown particle to him","His equation's extra solutions implied a positively charged electron twin","Experiments had shown that electrons can carry positive charge","He needed a heavier particle to explain how atoms hold together"], 1, "Dirac took the odd negative-energy solutions seriously, and they predicted antimatter."],
    [1, "What happens when a particle meets its antiparticle?", ["They bounce apart with swapped charges","They fuse into a single heavier particle","Their mass turns into energy, as gamma rays","About one per cent of mass becomes energy"], 2, "Annihilation is the most complete conversion of mass into energy known."],
    [2, "Why is antihydrogen so hard to store?", ["It repels ordinary matter, so it drifts away from any container","Touching any container wall destroys it, so magnetic fields hold it","It is radioactive and quickly melts through any container wall","It decays within a few nanoseconds whatever physicists try"], 1, "Antimatter annihilates on contact with matter, so it is held in a magnetic trap."],
    [3, "Why is antimatter not a practical fuel?", ["It releases less energy than fission does","It cannot be made in a laboratory at all","It is too stable to react with anything","It costs far more energy to make than it returns"], 3, "Making antiparticles in accelerators is staggeringly inefficient, so it is a research tool, not an energy source."],
    [4, "What is the puzzle of the missing antimatter?", ["Equal amounts should have formed, yet nearly everything is matter","Antimatter was made in equal amounts but is hidden inside stars","Matter and antimatter both survived in equal amounts today","No physical theory has ever predicted that antimatter exists"], 0, "Equal amounts would have annihilated, so a small excess of matter must have survived."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Every particle has a twin that destroys it. So why does anything exist?"});

